a long time senior official laid off by  one of Wall Street's top financial firms  is now suing accusing the company of  racial bias against black women Marilyn  Booker spent more than a quarter-century  at Morgan Stanley  she once led the firm's diversity and  inclusion program and now she claims in  her words that black lives do not matter  at Morgan Stanley now the firm strongly  disputes that national correspondent  Jericka Duncan spoke with Booker in her  first Network TV interview since filing  the lawsuit dareka Marilyn Booker has a  lot to say good morning to you good  morning Marilyn Booker used to work  right here at the headquarters and she  says listen she was an advocate for  black employees in fact in recent years  she had been pushing a program that  would allow for adequate training and  support for black financial managers so  that they could advance within the  company but Booker says she believes  those after and other things caused  Morgan Stanley to eliminate her position  Marilyn Booker recalled the day she  learned her 26-year career at Morgan  Stanley was over the Human Resources  person told me that my job was being  eliminated and the way they did it 26  years loyal employee I like to say that  I bled Morgan Stanley blue and they  fired me like I was a temp that was on  December 9th 2019 jobs are on the  chopping block at Morgan Stanley amid a  round of layoffs that Morgan Stanley  said included around 1,500 employees  more than 100 of which were managing  directors like Booker for 16 years  Booker served as the global head of  diversity I remember when I was in that  role the firm with respect to black  employees it was eight point eight  percent the last ten years under James  Gorman that number is now five point  eight percent what about you mr. Gorman  James Gorman took over as CEO in 2010  around the same time Booker was moved to  the wealth management division morgan  stanley disputes Booker's claim and says  the overall number of black employees  that the company has increased in the  last decade going from four point six  percent to five point eight percent and  number of black managing directors has  more than tripled but Booker says the  problem with diversity goes beyond  numbers they do not get the same  mentoring they don't get the same  support so they either fail out of the  program or they walk away in defeat  within months of proposing an idea to  address those problems Booker says she  was fired and this month she filed a  lawsuit bringing claims against Gorman  and managing director Barry Crouch for  race and gender discrimination  retaliation and unequal pay Booker's  attorney Jean Christensen I believe we  will be able to show that women at  Morgan Stanley in various positions are  underpaid compared to their male peers  but black female employees specifically  are discriminated against even worse why  do you think that is companies like  Morgan Stanley have been getting away  with this for years doing the right  thing alone isn't compelling enough for  most companies  Martin Davidson has been advising major  banks on diversity programs for over 15  years I have a hard time thinking about  anyone that has not dealt with this with  you sometimes dealing with these  lawsuits and settling them for what  seemed like large amounts of money for  them is just a cost of doing business  just one week after protests erupted  over the death of George Floyd Morgan  Stanley CEO sent an email to staff  acknowledging a turning point in race  relations and announcing 25 million  dollars to create an institute of  inclusion I wonder if this tragic thing  had not happened with George Floyd if  all of these epiphanies would have taken  place what outcome are you hoping for I  am hopeful that CEOs will take a look at  my case and then take a critical look at  their organizations  and ask themselves is writing a check to  the n-double-a-cp LDF enough is forming  a committee enough I want them to give  people the freedom to speak freely about  what the issues are and lastly I want  them to give black people a safe space  to be able to do that before they get to  the point where they have to either  leave the organization or they end up in  court with the organization Morgan's  family tells CBS this morning that it  strongly rejects the allegations in the  lawsuit of discrimination and unequal  pay the company emphasized that its new  inclusion Institute is just one of many  pieces of a broader and long-running  standing to try and address diversity in  fact they said they have 30 employees  that are dedicated to tackling those  specific issues but Booker's attorney  meanwhile said that since filing that  lawsuit she's received more than 10  calls from current and former employees  with similar complaints as Booker Tony  boy the whole thing calls to mind  Mellody Hobson suggestion that if you  want to change diversity numbers in  corporate America you've got to tie them  to executive pay money talks there and  elsewhere shreeka thank you very much
 

